  "result": "All 5 tests pass. The package is implemented in `bus/eventbus.py`.\n\nI chose Approach B, queued dispatch. It gives `flush()` a real job rather than making it a no-op, and it keeps handler-triggered publishes non-re-entrant with bounded stack depth, which matters once handlers start publishing to each other.\n\nImplementation notes:\n- Subscriptions are kept per topic in a list, so handlers run in subscription order. The returned unsubscribe function removes by identity and is safe to call twice.\n- Events go into a FIFO deque. Flush pops until empty, so events published during handling run after the current event's handlers complete. A nested flush call from inside a handler is a no-op, since the outer loop will drain it.\n- Dispatch iterates over a snapshot of the handler list, so subscribing or unsubscribing inside a handler does not disturb the event currently being handled.\n\nNothing has been committed. The working tree has the one new file.",
